---
/**
 * /nuclear-bomb-map/ — the rings on real ground.
 *
 * This page is the only place on the site that loads a map. The homepage keeps
 * its SVG ring diagram: that one survives scripting being off, and the two are
 * not substitutes for each other.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import BombMap from '../components/BombMap.astro';
import { YIELDS } from '../lib/effects';
import type { YieldKt } from '../lib/effects';
import { DEFAULT_CITY, cities, getCity } from '../lib/cities';

const city = getCity(DEFAULT_CITY) ?? cities[0];
const defaultYield: YieldKt = 100;
const defaultBurst = 'air' as const;
---

<Base
  title="Nuclear Bomb Map: Blast Rings on Real Ground, 20 Cities"
  description="Pick a city and a yield and see the rings on real ground, north-up and at true scale, with every distance traced to a printed page of the 1977 manual."
  path="/nuclear-bomb-map/"
  current="map"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script is:inline>
      document.documentElement.classList.add('js');
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <p class="eyebrow">Public-domain source · no model output</p>
  <h1>Nuclear Bomb Map — the rings on the ground, not on a chart</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The same five distances as the <a href="/blast-radius/">nuclear bomb blast radius</a> table,
    drawn where they actually land. Pick a city, pick a yield, and the rings are laid over the
    ground at true scale, north-up. Every number behind a ring still carries the figure and printed
    page it came from.
  </p>

  <BombMap city={city} y={defaultYield} burst={defaultBurst} cities={cities} />

  <p>
    The rings are geodesic circles — true ground distances — projected onto the basemap, so at 60°
    of latitude they are visibly not the same shape as at the equator. Each one is drawn from a
    gazetteer point outward, which means what you see is the distance from that point, not a
    stylised target. What the map does not know is the ground itself: hills, shorelines, a dense
    core of buildings and a field of the same beige are all one surface here, and the manual's
    distances assume flat terrain at sea level. Nothing on this page corrects for that.
  </p>

  <h2>What the 5 psi ring means</h2>

  <p>
    Five psi is the overpressure the manual ties to most residential buildings being destroyed. On
    this map that ring sits at 1.71&nbsp;km for 15&nbsp;kt, 3.22&nbsp;km for 100&nbsp;kt and
    6.93&nbsp;km for 1&nbsp;Mt, all of it from Fig.&nbsp;3.73c on p.&nbsp;115, read at 1&nbsp;kt and
    multiplied by the cube root of the yield. The chart prints 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10 psi contours and
    never 5, so each of those values is interpolated between the 4 and 6 psi curves: at 1&nbsp;kt
    the 5 psi position is 2,275&nbsp;ft from ground zero, at a burst height of 1,017&nbsp;ft. The
    quantity is peak overpressure on the ground, which already contains the reflected shock — not
    the incident pressure in free air — so a tool quoting incident pressure will draw a visibly
    smaller ring under the same label.
  </p>

  <h2>What the 1 psi ring means</h2>

  <p>
    One psi is the weakest contour the chart draws and the one that reaches furthest; it is roughly
    where window glass breaks. The ring measures 5.28&nbsp;km at 15&nbsp;kt, 9.93&nbsp;km (6.17&nbsp;mi)
    at 100&nbsp;kt and 21.40&nbsp;km (13.30&nbsp;mi) at 1&nbsp;Mt, again from Fig.&nbsp;3.73c,
    p.&nbsp;115. It is also the most sensitive of the five to burst height: at 1&nbsp;kt this
    contour reaches 7,020&nbsp;ft when the burst is at 1,575&nbsp;ft, against 3,830&nbsp;ft for a
    contact burst — a factor of 1.8 from burst height alone. That sensitivity is why every ring
    here is read at the peak of its own curve instead of at one burst height chosen in advance; the
    contact-burst convention is the one stated on p.&nbsp;106.
  </p>

  <h2>The fireball is not a thermal ring</h2>

  <p>
    The innermost ring is the fireball at the moment the shock front separates from it: 0.21&nbsp;km
    at 100&nbsp;kt, from R&nbsp;=&nbsp;110·W<sup>0.4</sup>&nbsp;ft, Eq.&nbsp;(2.127.1) on p.&nbsp;70.
    §2.129 notes it can later grow to about twice that radius, so the ring is a breakaway value, not
    a maximum. The thermal rings are a different claim: slant ranges to a given irradiance.
    4.5&nbsp;cal/cm² — second-degree burns on unshaded light skin — reaches 6.99&nbsp;km at
    100&nbsp;kt and 16.68&nbsp;km at 1&nbsp;Mt; 10&nbsp;cal/cm² — third-degree — 4.02&nbsp;km and
    10.35&nbsp;km, both read directly off Fig.&nbsp;7.42 (p.&nbsp;291), computed at
    200·W<sup>0.4</sup>&nbsp;ft with 12-mile visibility (§7.42, p.&nbsp;290). No thermal ring is
    drawn for a contact burst: the manual handles that case by a separate route (§7.41,
    p.&nbsp;290).
  </p>

  <h2>Why ten times the yield is not ten times the radius</h2>

  <p>
    For a given overpressure, distance scales as W<sup>1/3</sup> — the manual's own law,
    Eq.&nbsp;(3.61.2) on p.&nbsp;101, restated in §3.62 (p.&nbsp;102) and applied to these figures in
    §3.73 (p.&nbsp;110). From 100&nbsp;kt to 1&nbsp;Mt that factor is 10<sup>1/3</sup> = 2.15, and
    the map shows it: the 5 psi ring goes from 3.22&nbsp;km to 6.93&nbsp;km, a factor of 2.15; the
    1 psi ring from 9.93&nbsp;km to 21.40&nbsp;km, 2.16. Area grows with the square of the radius,
    so ten times the yield covers about 4.6 times the ground. That is why a 1&nbsp;Mt ring is not
    ten times wider than a 100&nbsp;kt ring, and why one zoom holds all three yields.
  </p>

  <h2>The 20 cities, and the population measure</h2>

  <p>
    The map offers 20 cities: New York, Los Angeles, Chicago, Houston, San Francisco, Washington,
    Miami, Seattle, Boston, Atlanta, London, Paris, Tokyo, Berlin, Sydney, Toronto, Mexico City,
    São Paulo, New Delhi and Johannesburg. Each centre is a GeoNames gazetteer point, not an
    administrative centroid: boundaries differ by country, so rings would not be comparable. A city
    is a place for the numbers, not a new set: every ring comes from Fig.&nbsp;3.73c (p.&nbsp;115)
    for blast and fireball, Fig.&nbsp;7.42 (p.&nbsp;291) for thermal. Population is not printed here
    yet; the convention is fixed — residents inside 10&nbsp;km of that point on the WorldPop
    1&nbsp;km grid, 2020 estimates, with radius, year and dataset beside the number. Ring
    populations are a separate count, never merged into the city total. One city at a time, rings
    intact without scripting: the <a href="/">nuke simulator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where these numbers stop being readable</h2>

  <p>
    Three cells here sit at the edge of what Fig.&nbsp;3.73c supports. Reading each contour at its
    peak puts the 1&nbsp;Mt burst heights at 10,174&nbsp;ft for 5 psi and 15,750&nbsp;ft for
    1 psi, and even the 100&nbsp;kt 1 psi ring at 7,311&nbsp;ft. The chart is computed for a
    homogeneous sea-level atmosphere at burst heights of 5,000&nbsp;ft or less (§3.73, p.&nbsp;110),
    so those rings extrapolate its assumption by up to a factor of three. They stay: dropping
    1&nbsp;Mt would hide the limit, not state it. No altitude correction (§3.64–3.66) is applied,
    which pushes them low, not high. Read them as the edge of what the chart supports; the trail is
    under <a href="/methodology/">where these numbers come from</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="small">
    Yields covered: {YIELDS.map((y) => `${y.toLocaleString('en-US')} kt`).join(' · ')}.
    Cities: {cities.length}. Contact bursts are shown for the fireball and the two overpressure
    rings; thermal radii are not drawn for a contact burst, because Fig.&nbsp;7.42 is an air-burst
    chart and the manual handles surface-burst heating by a separate route (§7.41, p.&nbsp;290).
  </p>
</Base>
